  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-3x+4<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3x+4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+3x-4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+4)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/6a5528147c994e180c2ffb41bd259dae489a3fc02fa1d6c77452d6cf1722e9be.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-4$ or $x>1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
